WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
Batch normalization.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.709
As every layer updates at once, the inputs to each layer keep shifting.

3
00:00:06.709 --> 00:00:08.200
BatchNorm steadies them.

4
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.800
For each feature, we normalize across the current minibatch to mean zero and variance one.

5
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.900
Then two learned parameters let the network undo that wherever it helps.

6
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.500
Here's one feature across a batch of four.

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.534
The raw values are two, four, six, and eight.

8
00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:26.567
The mean is five.

9
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:30.467
After normalizing, they're centered on zero with a spread of one.

10
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:34.933
After standardizing, we apply a learned scale and shift.

11
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:36.400
Gamma rescales.

12
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:37.630
And beta shifts.

13
00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:41.933
Layers train against a steady input, so you can use higher learning rates.

14
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:43.972
Now the trap.

15
00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:46.167
BatchNorm is really two functions.

16
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:52.067
In training, it uses the current batch's statistics, while quietly keeping running averages.

17
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:56.211
At inference, it must use those frozen running statistics.

18
00:00:56.211 --> 00:00:58.067
A single image has no batch.

19
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.419
Model dot eval flips the switch.

20
00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:02.600
Forget it, and predictions wobble.

21
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.767
Let's reproduce the bug on purpose.

22
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:08.367
BatchNorm sits right after the linear layer.

23
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.000
We stay in train mode.

24
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.033
The same curve gives different answers depending on its batch neighbours.

25
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:16.267
After eval, the results match.

26
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:19.400
Here's how this shows up in production.

27
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:24.833
Your classify endpoint returns zero point nine one for a light curve on its own.

28
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:29.200
The same curve in a batch of requests scores zero point seven eight.

29
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.859
Nothing random is on.

30
00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:35.002
A missing eval call after loading the checkpoint is the culprit.

31
00:01:35.002 --> 00:01:36.167
One line fixes it.

32
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:38.154
Let's recap.

33
00:01:38.154 --> 00:01:41.500
BatchNorm normalizes each feature across the batch.

34
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.133
Gamma and beta restore flexibility.

35
00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:47.133
Training and inference use different statistics.

36
00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:50.105
So always call eval before predicting.

37
00:01:50.105 --> 00:01:51.300
Next up: dropout.
